in this lesson we're going to talk about  special right triangles so there's two  special right triangles you want to  familiarize yourself with and that's the  45-45-90  as well as the 30-60-90 so these are the  ratios that you want to memorize in a  45-45-90 triangle the two legs are the  two sides that make up the right angle  those are going to be the same because  this is an isosceles triangle but the  hypotenuse that's going to be  the leg times the square root of 2. so  square root of 2 is about 1.4 so the  hypotenuse is going to be the longer  side and it's going to be square root of  2 times  longer and if they give you the  hypotenuse what you would do is you  would then divide by the square root of  2 to get back to the to the leg or the  side and these are both going to be the  same  so the 45 45 90 there's not as much to  remember the 30 60 90 though what you  want to remember is that the side across  from the 30 degree angle that's going to  be the short leg that leg we call x the  one across from the 60 degree angle  that's the medium length side and that's  going to be x times the square root of 3  and then the longest side is across from  the height the right angle that's the  hypotenuse that's 2x  so essentially what you want to remember  is that if you have this side you just  have to double it to get the hypotenuse  or if you have the hypotenuse just  divide it by two to get back to the  short leg and then if you have the short  leg you can multiply by square root of  three to get the longer leg or divide by  square root of three to get the shorter  leg so let's go through some examples so  you can see how this works so let's do  the 45 45 90 ones first  so if both of these are five okay that  tells you that this is isosceles which  means that these are the same and these  are both going to be the same so this is  a 45 45 90 triangle  so we know this is five so that means  that x must be five times the square  root of two easy right okay now this  one's a little bit more challenging we  know that these sides are congruent we  can see that this is going to be a  45-45-90 triangle because all the angles  have to add up to 180 right  but to get from this hypotenuse to the  leg we actually have to do the opposite  we have to divide by the square root of  2. you don't want the square root in the  denominator so you rationalize by  multiplying by square root of 2 over  square root of 2. so that's going to  give you 10 square root of 2 square root  of 2 times square root of 2 is square  root of 4 which is 2 and the 10 and the  2 you can reduce that gives you 5 square  root of 2. so that would be the length  of this side as well as this side  okay now going over to the 30 60 90  triangle  that's this one here here they're giving  us the short leg so this is pretty easy  all we have to do is multiply by the  square root of 3  to get the longer leg and then double to  get the hypotenuse so that's going to be  8.  in this example here you can see it's a  30-60-90 triangle they're giving us the  hypotenuse we have to divide by two so  that's going to be six for the short leg  and then once we know the short leg we  can multiply by the square root of three  to get the longer leg so six square root  of three  and then the last example this is  probably the toughest example in a  30-60-90 triangle they're giving you the  longer leg that's the x squared of 3  side we want to divide by the square  root of 3 to get the shorter leg and  then we can double it to get the  hypotenuse so let's do that so 9 divided  by square root of 3 we don't want the  square root of 3 in the denominator so  we're going to rationalize by  multiplying by square root of 3 over  square root of 3. so that gives us 9  square root of 3  over 3.  9 divided by 3 is 3 so that's 3 square  root of 3. so that's the shorter leg  right here and then if we double it by  multiplying by 2 we get 6 square root of  3 is the hypotenuse okay now let's do a  more  typical example of what you may see  on the a-c-t  so for example here they give us this  45-45-90 combined with a 30-60-90  triangle making a little bit more  challenging and we're trying to find out  this length here y so go ahead and pause  the video see if you can solve this on  your own we'll go through it together  so what i would do here is um we know  that this is a 45 45 90. so if this is 3  this is also going to be 3 and then the  hypotenuse here is going to be 3 square  root of 2 okay  but now they're giving us the longer leg  in this 30-60-90 triangle so to get to  the shorter leg over here we actually  have to divide by the square root of 3.  we don't want that square root of 3 in  the denominator so i'm going to  rationalize so that gives us 3 square  root of 6 over 3 okay and you can see  the 3s are canceling so this is just  going to be square root of 6.  now to get the hypotenuse all we have to  do now is double  so that's going to be 2 times the square  root of 6  and  that's the length of the of the  hypotenuse so the 3690 triangle and  that's what we're solving for so you got  it so definitely something important to  know when you're preparing for the act  are these two special right triangles  memorize the ratios and if you need to  review this lesson i'll see you in the  next lesson
 

